scientists advising the world health  organization say the astrazeneca vaccine  should be given to all adults over 18  with no  upper age limit the recommendation comes  after a number of countries opted not to  give the jab to those  over the age of 65 because of doubts  over its effectiveness  and south africa suspended its rollout  the who's chair of the strategic  advisory group of  experts on immunization dr alejandro  craviotto  had this to say since we have identified  people over 65 was one of our priority  groups  in the prioritization roadmap we  have based a totality of  looking at the safety and the  immunogenicity data  and therefore we recommend for the  vaccine to be used  in people 18 years and above without an  upper age limit that means people over  65 years of age  should be given the vaccination well  let's bring in our science correspondent  thomas moore now put this into context  for us why has the world health  organization had to issue this guidance  thomas  well i think all countries were looking  to the world health organization to do  just that because  the vaccine is now available and they  wanted the best available  recommendations for its use and i think  what they have  essentially decided is that yes there  are gaps  in the data there is a limit to the  copper bottoms evidence that comes from  clinical trials but  filling in the gaps they do believe that  the vaccine is effective in  the older patients and that it should be  given between  eight and uh with a a dose interval of  eight to twelve weeks as it is being  given here in the uk  and then it should be given even though  uh there may be variants that may reduce  its effectiveness now on all three  points let's go back to the first one  the elderly of course uh president  macron of france he has said that  the vaccine was quasi-ineffective the  german vaccine  advisers uh said that it shouldn't be  used in the elderly until there's  stronger evidence  but the double eho has very much sided  with the uk  and how it's being used here and after  days  of of uh really quite uh uh  strong uh criticism of the way the  vaccine is being used that perhaps  undermine public confidence this is a  a strong vote of confidence for the  astrazeneca oxford vaccine
 

